      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, to some degree, it's not simply about training and infrastructure, but also involves deeper cultural work values and attitudes towards work. European work culture often emphasizes punctuality, reliability, and a thorough approach to quality control, which aligns well with the demands of a high-autonomy, high-pressure environment because workers are conditioned to understand and meet rigorous, consistent standards.\n\nFurthermore, European work cultures tend to include stronger regulatory frameworks and labor laws that incentivize and enforce high standards of professionalism in the workplace. In contrast, high-growth, lower-cost regions like those in Sub-Saharan Africa or Southeast Asia may prioritize rapid completion of tasks over precision due to their growth strategies and business climates, which can lead to a more variable work ethic. It's not that these regions can't handle the same level of autonomy and problem-solving, but they may need additional systems, such as robust oversight and continuous training, to achieve the precision expected in high-autonomy environments. This can increase the required investment in terms of training, time, and resources to ensure that work culture in those regions aligns with the high precision standards set out by your production line.",
      "source": "target",
